<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Assignment 4: Three-Dimensional Space, Vectors and the Dot Product.

  Built from the problems marked A4 in the instructor's markup of the old
  worksheets "Exercises for Three-Dimensional Coordinate Systems",
  "Exercises for Vectors" and "Exercises for The Dot Product".  The problems
  marked R2 in the same markup went to review-problems-2.ptx.

  Notation: the markup asks for double bars for the length of a vector
  everywhere, so |v| has become \|v\| throughout, and rw17-4 now states the
  scalar component as \|proj_d v\| rather than with comp_d.
  Figure work requested by the markup: fig-rw22-bisector dashes the part of
  segment AB that passes behind the plane and closes the right-angle marker at
  C; fig-dp-constrained-work closes the outline of the plane.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 4</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems set up coordinates in space, describe regions and solids by
equations and inequalities, do algebra with vectors, and use the dot product
to measure angles and to split a vector into a piece along a given direction
and a piece orthogonal to it. If you would like to review the material first,
see
<xref ref="chapter-three-dimensional-coordinates" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="chapter-vectors" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="section-dot-product" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-3">
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find an equation of the set of all points equidistant from the points
<m>A(-1,5,3)</m> and <m>B(6,2,-2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the geometrical shape corresponding to the set.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We want to find all points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> that have the same distance
from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Squaring both distances (which is
equivalent, since distances are non-negative) gives
<md>
(x+1)^2 + (y-5)^2 + (z-3)^2 = (x-6)^2 + (y-2)^2 + (z+2)^2.
</md>
Expanding both sides,
<md>
<mrow>\amp x^2 + 2x + 1 + y^2 - 10y + 25 + z^2 - 6z + 9</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = x^2 - 12x + 36 + y^2 - 4y + 4 + z^2 + 4z + 4,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp 2x - 10y - 6z + 35 = -12x - 4y + 4z + 44,</mrow>
</md>
and collecting terms,
<md>
14x - 6y - 10z = 9.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
This is the equation of a plane. Its normal vector is
<m>\langle 14, -6, -10\rangle = 2\langle 7, -3, -5\rangle</m>, which
is parallel to
<m>\vec{AB} = \langle 6-(-1),\, 2-5,\, -2-3\rangle
= \langle 7, -3, -5\rangle</m>. Moreover, the midpoint of
<m>\overline{AB}</m>,
<md>
C\left(\frac{-1+6}{2},\, \frac{5+2}{2},\, \frac{3+(-2)}{2}\right)
= C\left(\frac52, \frac72, \frac12\right),
</md>
satisfies the equation, since
<m>14\left(\frac52\right) - 6\left(\frac72\right)
- 10\left(\frac12\right) = 35 - 21 - 5 = 9</m>. Hence the set is the
plane that passes through the midpoint of <m>\overline{AB}</m> and is
orthogonal to <m>\vec{AB}</m>, i.e. the <term>perpendicular bisector
plane</term> of the segment <m>\overline{AB}</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw22-bisector"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw22-bisector">
<caption>
The set of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is the plane
through the midpoint <m>C</m> of <m>\overline{AB}</m> perpendicular to
<m>\vec{AB}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
Two points A and B are joined by a segment. A shaded rectangle
representing a plane cuts the segment at right angles at its midpoint
C, with a small right angle marker at C. The stretch of the segment
that passes behind the plane is drawn dashed.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % the plane, drawn as a slanted rectangle
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.25, rotate=-28] (-1.15,-1.9) rectangle (1.15,1.9);
  \draw[mred, thick, rotate=-28] (-1.15,-1.9) rectangle (1.15,1.9);
  % the segment AB: the stretch from where it enters the plane patch up to
  % the midpoint C runs behind the plane, so it is drawn dashed
  \draw[thick] (-2.4,-0.6) -- (-1.15,-0.287);
  \draw[thick, dashed] (-1.15,-0.287) -- (0,0);
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2.4,0.6);
  \fill (-2.4,-0.6) circle (2pt) node[left=2pt] {$A$};
  \fill (2.4,0.6) circle (2pt) node[right=2pt] {$B$};
  % the midpoint
  \fill[mblue] (0,0) circle (2pt);
  \node[mblue, above left=1pt] at (0,0.06) {$C$};
  % right-angle marker at C, closed back onto the segment AB
  \draw[mred] (0.22,0.055) -- (0.28,-0.185) -- (0.06,-0.24) -- (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-24">
<statement>
<p>
Describe the following regions and graphs.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4</m>, <m>z \ge 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4</m>, <m>z = 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4</m>, <m>z \ge 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4</m>, <m>z = 0</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The key is to notice whether the first condition is an
<em>inequality</em> (a solid region) or an <em>equation</em> (a
surface), and then what the second condition does to it.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The solid ball of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin, restricted
to <m>z \ge 0</m>: the <em>upper half of the solid ball</em>,
including the flat disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>z = 0</m> in the solid ball gives <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>:
the <em>solid disk</em> of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin,
lying in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The sphere of radius <m>2</m> restricted to <m>z \ge 0</m>: the
<em>upper hemisphere</em> (a surface only), together with the circle
where it meets the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>z = 0</m> on the sphere gives <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>: the
<em>circle</em> of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin in the
<m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m1-3">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the point <m>(3,7,-5)</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
What is its distance to the <m>yz</m>-plane?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is its distance to the <m>y</m>-axis?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The <m>yz</m>-plane is the plane <m>x = 0</m>. The
closest point of that plane to <m>(3,7,-5)</m> is obtained by simply
setting the <m>x</m>-coordinate to zero, giving <m>(0,7,-5)</m>. Hence
the distance is
<md>
\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (7-7)^2 + (-5+5)^2} = |3| = 3 .
</md>
In general the distance from <m>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> to the
<m>yz</m>-plane is <m>|x_0|</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The <m>y</m>-axis consists of the points
<m>(0,y,0)</m>. The closest such point to <m>(3,7,-5)</m> is the one
with the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, namely <m>(0,7,0)</m>, so the
distance is
<md>
\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (7-7)^2 + (-5-0)^2} = \sqrt{9+25} = \sqrt{34} .
</md>
In general the distance from <m>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> to the
<m>y</m>-axis is <m>\sqrt{x_0^2+z_0^2}</m>: we drop the coordinate
along the axis and keep the other two.
See <xref ref="fig-m1-distances"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m1-distances">
<caption>
The point <m>P(3,7,-5)</m>, its distance <m>3</m> to the
<m>yz</m>-plane (measured horizontally, parallel to the
<m>x</m>-axis), and its distance <m>\sqrt{34}</m> to the
<m>y</m>-axis (measured perpendicular to that axis).
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system with the point P at three, seven,
negative five. A segment runs from P horizontally to the point zero,
seven, negative five in the y z plane, and a second segment runs from P
to the point zero, seven, zero on the y axis.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (-1,0,0) -- (5,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1,0) -- (0,9,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-7) -- (0,0,3) node[above] {$z$};
% a patch of the yz-plane
\fill[mblue, opacity=0.14] (0,3.4,-6.8) -- (0,9.2,-6.8) -- (0,9.2,1.6)
      -- (0,3.4,1.6) -- cycle;
\node[mblue, anchor=south] at (0,6.3,1.7) {$yz$-plane};
% the point and its shadows
\coordinate (P) at (3,7,-5);
\coordinate (F) at (0,7,-5);
\coordinate (A) at (0,7,0);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (0,7,0) -- (F);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (3,0,0) -- (3,7,0) -- (P);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (3,7,0) -- (A);
\draw[very thick, mverm] (P) -- (F)
      node[midway, anchor=north west, inner sep=1pt] {$3$};
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (P) -- (A)
      node[midway, anchor=east] {$\sqrt{34}$\;};
\fill[mred] (P) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[mverm] (F) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[mgreen] (A) circle (1.6pt);
\node[mred, anchor=north east] at (P) {$P(3,7,-5)$\;};
\node[mverm, anchor=west] at (F) {\;$(0,7,-5)$};
\node[mgreen, anchor=south west] at (A) {\;$(0,7,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-nonconvex-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Hemisphere, a Plane, and a Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid region bounded below by the hemisphere
<m>z = -\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>, above by the plane <m>z=1</m>, and on the
outside by the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. Describe this solid.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Squaring <m>z=-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> gives <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>, and the
minus sign keeps only the points with <m>z \le 0</m>: the surface is the
lower half of the unit sphere centered at the origin, with its lowest
point at <m>(0,0,-1)</m>. It reaches <m>z=0</m> exactly on the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, the same circle where the cylinder meets the
<m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
<p>
The solid consists of the points inside the cylinder that lie
<em>above</em> the hemisphere and <em>below</em> the plane:
<md>
x^2+y^2 \le 1, \qquad -\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2} \le z \le 1.
</md>
At the center <m>(x,y)=(0,0)</m>, this range for <m>z</m> is the full
interval <m>-1 \le z \le 1</m>. At the rim <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, it shrinks
to <m>0 \le z \le 1</m>. The part with <m>z \ge 0</m> is the solid
cylinder <m>x^2+y^2\le1</m>, <m>0\le z\le 1</m>, and the part with
<m>z \le 0</m> is the solid lower half of the unit ball,
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2 \le 1</m>, <m>z \le 0</m>. The solid is therefore a can
of radius <m>1</m> and height <m>1</m> with a hemispherical bowl
attached beneath it along the unit disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane, like
the rounded end of a test tube, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-nonconvex-solid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-nonconvex-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded below by the hemisphere
<m>z=-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>, above by the plane <m>z=1</m>, and on the
outside by the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>: a solid cylinder of height
<m>1</m> sitting on a solid half-ball, the two joined along the unit
disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<description>
<p>
A short solid cylinder whose bottom is closed off by a rounded bowl.
The flat top of the cylinder lies in the plane z equals 1 and its side
is the cylinder x squared plus y squared equals 1. Below the xy-plane
the solid continues as the lower half of the unit sphere, reaching down
to the point zero, zero, negative one, like the rounded end of a test
tube.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=2.0]
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
\coordinate (A) at (1.25,1.25,1);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.25,1.25,1);
\coordinate (C) at (-1.25,-1.5,1);
\coordinate (D) at (1.25,-1.5,1);
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (1.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-1.25,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,1.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-1.25,0);
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-1.0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1.0);
% the hemisphere z = -sqrt(1-x^2-y^2): meridians
\foreach \angulo in {0,4,...,356}{
  \draw[cyan,very thick,rotate around z=\angulo,opacity=0.15] plot[domain=0:90,smooth,variable=\a] ({0},{sin(\a)},{-cos(\a)});
}
% the hemisphere: circles of constant height
\foreach \polar in {6,12,...,84}{
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:360,smooth,variable=\t] ({sin(\polar)*cos(\t)},{sin(\polar)*sin(\t)},{-cos(\polar)});
}
% the curves slicing the hemisphere
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-90:90,smooth,variable=\a] ({sin(\a)},0,{-cos(\a)});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-90:90,smooth,variable=\a] (0,{sin(\a)},{-cos(\a)});
\fill[blue] (0,0,-1) circle (0.6pt) node[below=1pt] {\footnotesize$(0,0,-1)$};
\node[blue,right] at (0,1.0,-0.6) {$z = -\sqrt{1 - x^2 - y^2}$};
% the unit disk where the hemisphere meets the cylinder
\fill[yellow,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[black,thick,opacity=0.75] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% the outer cylinder wall
\foreach \angulo in {0,0.01,...,\final}{
  \pgfmathparse{cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[gray,opacity=0.5] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,1.0);
}
\node[black,right] at (0,1.0,0.5) {$x^2 + y^2 = 1$};
% the circle at z = 1
\draw[black,thick,opacity=0.75] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{1.0});
% the plane z = 1
\draw[white] (C) -- (B) node[red,above,sloped,midway]{\footnotesize$z = 1$};
\draw[red,dash dot] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pattern color=pink,pattern=north east lines] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,1.0) -- (0,0,1.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-2">
<statement>
<p>
Write each combination of vectors as a single vector. See
<xref ref="fig-rs19-quad"/>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{PQ} + \vec{QR}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{QS} - \vec{PS}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{RS} + \vec{SP} + \vec{PQ}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-quad">
<caption>
The quadrilateral with vertices <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, <m>R</m>, and
<m>S</m>, with its diagonals.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rs19-quad">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-0.55,3.9,3.5)">
<line at="qd-QP" endpoints="((0.8,2.9),(2.9,2.2))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-PS" endpoints="((2.9,2.2),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-RS" endpoints="((0,0),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-RQ" endpoints="((0,0),(0.8,2.9))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-QS" endpoints="((0.8,2.9),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="qd-RP" endpoints="((0,0),(2.9,2.2))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="qd-P" p="(2.9,2.2)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-Q" p="(0.8,2.9)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-R" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-S" p="(3.3,0.3)" fill="black"/>
<label at="qd-P-lab" anchor="(3.0,2.32)" alignment="ne">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-Q-lab" anchor="(0.72,3.02)" alignment="nw">
<m>Q</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-R-lab" anchor="(-0.12,-0.12)" alignment="sw">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-S-lab" anchor="(3.42,0.22)" alignment="e">
<m>S</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A quadrilateral with vertices labelled Q at the top left, P at the top right, R at the bottom left, and S at the bottom right, drawn in light blue together with its two diagonals Q S and R P.">
  <annotation ref="qd-P" text="The vertex P at the top right."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-Q" text="The vertex Q at the top left."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-R" text="The vertex R at the bottom left."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-S" text="The vertex S at the bottom right."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
By the triangle law, placing <m>\vec{QR}</m> at the tip of
<m>\vec{PQ}</m>,
<md>
\vec{PQ} + \vec{QR} = \vec{PR}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Since <m>-\vec{PS} = \vec{SP}</m>,
<md>
\vec{QS} - \vec{PS} = \vec{QS} + \vec{SP} = \vec{QP}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Chaining the three vectors tip to tail,
<md>
\vec{RS} + \vec{SP} + \vec{PQ} = \vec{RQ}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-9">
<statement>
<p>
Find a vector of length 3 pointing in the opposite direction from the
vector <m>2\,\mathbf i - 4\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The given vector has length
<m>\sqrt{4 + 16 + 16} = \sqrt{36} = 6</m>, so the unit vector in its
direction is <m>\frac16\langle 2, -4, 4\rangle
= \left\langle \frac13, -\frac23, \frac23\right\rangle</m>.
Multiplying by <m>-3</m> reverses the direction and gives length
<m>3</m>:
<md>
-3\left\langle \frac13, -\frac23, \frac23\right\rangle
= \langle -1, 2, -2\rangle
= -\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m1-2">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\vec v</m> be the vector that goes from the point
<m>\left(5,-4,\sqrt3\right)</m> to the point <m>(3,-1,0)</m>. Find a
vector of length <m>\tfrac13</m> that points in the same direction as
<m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The vector from <m>P(5,-4,\sqrt3)</m> to <m>Q(3,-1,0)</m> is obtained
by subtracting the tail from the head:
<md>
\vec v = \vec{PQ} = \left\langle 3-5,\ -1-(-4),\ 0-\sqrt3
\right\rangle = \left\langle -2,\ 3,\ -\sqrt3 \right\rangle .
</md>
</p>

<p>
Its length is
<md>
\|\vec v\| = \sqrt{(-2)^2 + 3^2 + \left(-\sqrt3\right)^2}
           = \sqrt{4+9+3} = \sqrt{16} = 4 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
The unit vector in the direction of <m>\vec v</m> is therefore
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\vec v}{\|\vec v\|}
       = \frac14 \left\langle -2,\ 3,\ -\sqrt3 \right\rangle .
</md>
Scaling by <m>\tfrac13</m> gives a vector of length <m>\tfrac13</m>
pointing the same way:
<md>
\frac13\,\hat u = \frac{1}{12}\left\langle -2,\ 3,\ -\sqrt3
\right\rangle
= \left\langle -\frac16,\ \frac14,\ -\frac{\sqrt3}{12}
\right\rangle .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="vec-parallelogram-space">
<title>A parallelogram in space</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the four points
<md>
A(1,2,5), \qquad B(4,3,3), \qquad C(6,7,4), \qquad D(3,6,6).
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Show that <m>ABCD</m> is a parallelogram.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the point <m>M</m> at which the two diagonals of <m>ABCD</m> meet.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the perimeter of <m>ABCD</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
A quadrilateral is a parallelogram exactly when one pair of opposite
sides is given by the <em>same</em> vector, since equal vectors have both
the same direction and the same length. Along the sides <m>AB</m> and
<m>DC</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AB} \amp = \langle 4-1,\; 3-2,\; 3-5 \rangle
= \langle 3,\; 1,\; -2 \rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\vec{DC} \amp = \langle 6-3,\; 7-6,\; 4-6 \rangle
= \langle 3,\; 1,\; -2 \rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Since <m>\vec{AB} = \vec{DC}</m>, these two sides are parallel and have
equal length, so <m>ABCD</m> is a parallelogram. The other pair of sides
matches as well:
<md>
\vec{AD} = \langle 2,\; 4,\; 1 \rangle = \vec{BC}.
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-vec-parallelogram-space"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so <m>M</m> is the
midpoint of <m>AC</m>. Using position vectors and
<m>\vec{AC} = \langle 5,\; 5,\; -1 \rangle</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\vec{OM} \amp = \vec{OA} + \tfrac12 \vec{AC}
= \langle 1,\; 2,\; 5 \rangle
+ \tfrac12\langle 5,\; 5,\; -1 \rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle \tfrac72,\; \tfrac92,\; \tfrac92
\right\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Travelling along the other diagonal instead, with
<m>\vec{BD} = \langle -1,\; 3,\; 3\rangle</m>,
<md>
\vec{OB} + \tfrac12 \vec{BD}
= \langle 4,\; 3,\; 3 \rangle + \tfrac12\langle -1,\; 3,\; 3 \rangle
= \left\langle \tfrac72,\; \tfrac92,\; \tfrac92 \right\rangle,
</md>
the same point, which confirms both the answer and part A. Hence
<m>M\left(\frac72,\, \frac92,\, \frac92\right)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Opposite sides have equal length, so the perimeter is
<m>2\left(\left\|\vec{AB}\right\| + \left\|\vec{AD}\right\|\right)</m>, where
<md>
<mrow>\left\|\vec{AB}\right\| \amp = \sqrt{3^2 + 1^2 + (-2)^2}
= \sqrt{14},</mrow>
<mrow>\left\|\vec{AD}\right\| \amp = \sqrt{2^2 + 4^2 + 1^2}
= \sqrt{21}.</mrow>
</md>
The perimeter is therefore
<m>2\left(\sqrt{14} + \sqrt{21}\right) \approx 16.65</m>. Since
<m>\sqrt{14} \ne \sqrt{21}</m>, this parallelogram is not a rhombus.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-parallelogram-space">
<caption>
The parallelogram <m>ABCD</m>, with
<m>\vec{AB} = \vec{DC} = \langle 3,1,-2\rangle</m> drawn in blue and
<m>\vec{AD} = \vec{BC} = \langle 2,4,1\rangle</m> in magenta. The
diagonals <m>AC</m> and <m>BD</m> cross at their common midpoint
<m>M\left(\frac72,\frac92,\frac92\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
Three coordinate axes labelled x, y and z. Four points A, B, C and D in
space are joined into a slanted parallelogram. The two sides from A to B
and from D to C are drawn as blue arrows of the same length and
direction, and the two sides from A to D and from B to C are drawn as
magenta arrows, also equal to each other. The two diagonals, from A to C
and from B to D, are dashed grey segments that cross at a marked red
point M in the middle of the parallelogram.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.78, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (5.0,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,8.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,7.2) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above left] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
\coordinate (A) at (1,2,5);
\coordinate (B) at (4,3,3);
\coordinate (C) at (6,7,4);
\coordinate (D) at (3,6,6);
\coordinate (M) at (3.5,4.5,4.5);
% the diagonals, drawn first so the sides sit on top of them
\draw[gray, thick, dashed] (A) -- (C);
\draw[gray, thick, dashed] (B) -- (D);
% the two pairs of equal sides
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (D) -- (C);
\draw[->, magenta, very thick] (A) -- (D);
\draw[->, magenta, very thick] (B) -- (C);
% vertices
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D} \fill[black] (\p) circle (1.7pt);
\fill[red] (M) circle (2.0pt);
\node[left=3pt]         at (A) {$A(1,2,5)$};
\node[below left=1pt]   at (B) {$B(4,3,3)$};
\node[below right=1pt]  at (C) {$C(6,7,4)$};
\node[above right=1pt]  at (D) {$D(3,6,6)$};
\node[red, right=2pt]   at (M) {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-4">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two vectors
<m>\mathbf u = 3\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \mathbf i + \mathbf j</m>. Write <m>\mathbf u</m> as
the sum of a vector parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m> and a vector
orthogonal to <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>\vec u \cdot \vec v = 3</m> and
<m>\|\vec v\| = \sqrt{1+1} = \sqrt2</m>. The component of
<m>\vec u</m> parallel to <m>\vec v</m> is
<md>
\vec u_{\parallel}
= \operatorname{proj}_{\vec v} \vec u
= \left(\frac{\vec u \cdot \vec v}{\|\vec v\|^2}\right)\vec v
= \frac32\,(\mathbf i + \mathbf j)
= \frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j,
</md>
and the component orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m> is
<md>
\vec u_{\perp}
= \vec u - \vec u_{\parallel}
= \left(3\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k\right)
- \left(\frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j\right)
= -\frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k.
</md>
Here <m>\vec u_{\parallel}</m> is parallel to <m>\vec v</m> and
<m>\vec u_{\perp}</m> is orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m>. Note that
<m>\vec u_{\parallel} + \vec u_{\perp} = \vec u</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-4">
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the following pairs of vectors are orthogonal,
parallel, or neither.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec u_1 = -3\,\mathbf i + 9\,\mathbf j + 6\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\vec u_2 = 4\,\mathbf i - 12\,\mathbf j - 8\,\mathbf k</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec v_1 = \mathbf i + \mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\vec v_2 = \mathbf i + \mathbf j + \mathbf k</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec w_1 = a\,\mathbf i + b\,\mathbf j</m> and
<m>\vec w_2 = c\,\mathbf k</m>, where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>
are non-zero real numbers.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<term>Parallel</term>, since <m>\vec u_2 = -\frac43\,\vec u_1</m>:
indeed
<m>-\frac43\langle -3, 9, 6\rangle = \langle 4, -12, -8\rangle</m>.
(Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the
other.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<term>Orthogonal</term>, since
<m>\vec v_1 \cdot \vec v_2 = 1 + 1 - 2 = 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<term>Orthogonal</term>, since
<m>\vec w_1 \cdot \vec w_2
= \langle a, b, 0\rangle \cdot \langle 0, 0, c\rangle = 0</m>. They
are not parallel, because <m>\vec w_2</m> has a non-zero
<m>\mathbf k</m> component while <m>\vec w_1</m> does not.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-4">
<statement>
<p>
A 100-meter dash is run on a track in the direction of the vector
<m>2\,\mathbf i + 6\,\mathbf j</m>. The wind velocity is
<m>\mathbf v = 5\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf j</m> km/hr. The rules say
that a legal wind speed measured in the direction of the dash must not
exceed 5 km/hr.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Will the race results be disqualified due to an illegal wind? Justify
your answer.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the velocity vector of the wind in the direction of the dash.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>\vec d = \langle 2, 6\rangle</m> be the direction of the dash
and <m>\vec v = \langle 5, 2\rangle</m> the wind velocity. Then
<md>
\vec v \cdot \vec d = 10 + 12 = 22,
\qquad
\|\vec d\| = \sqrt{4 + 36} = \sqrt{40} = 2\sqrt{10}.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The wind speed measured in the direction of the dash is the length of
the projection of <m>\vec v</m> onto <m>\vec d</m>:
<md>
\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec d}\vec v\right\|
= \frac{\vec v \cdot \vec d}{\|\vec d\|}
= \frac{22}{2\sqrt{10}}
= \frac{11}{\sqrt{10}} \approx 3.48 \text{ km/hr}.
</md>
Since <m>3.48 \lt 5</m>, the wind is legal and the race results will
<em>not</em> be disqualified.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The velocity vector of the wind in the direction of the dash is the
vector projection
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec d}\vec v
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec v \cdot \vec d}{\|\vec d\|^2}\right)\vec d
= \frac{22}{40}\langle 2, 6\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle \frac{11}{10}, \frac{33}{10}\right\rangle
= 1.1\,\mathbf i + 3.3\,\mathbf j \text{ km/hr}.</mrow>
</md>
As a check, its length is
<m>\sqrt{1.1^2 + 3.3^2} = 1.1\sqrt{10} = \frac{11}{\sqrt{10}}</m>,
matching part A.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dp-constrained-work">
<statement>
<p>
A constant force is
<md>
\mathbf F = \left\langle 2, -1, 3 \right\rangle .
</md>
A particle is constrained to move in directions parallel to the plane
<md>
2x - y + 2z = 0 .
</md>
For a displacement of one unit, the work done by the force is
<m>W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d</m>, where <m>\mathbf d</m> is an
allowed unit direction lying in the plane.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find the unit direction in the plane that maximizes the work.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum possible work per unit distance.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find one unit direction in the plane for which the work is zero.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Reading the coefficients of the plane, a normal vector is
<md>
\mathbf n = \left\langle 2, -1, 2 \right\rangle ,
\qquad \|\mathbf n\| = \sqrt{4+1+4} = 3 .
</md>
A vector is parallel to the plane exactly when it is orthogonal to
<m>\mathbf n</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> Decompose the force into a normal piece and a piece
lying in the plane,
<md>
\mathbf F = \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
          + \mathbf F_{\parallel} .
</md>
The first piece comes from the projection formula. Since
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= 2(2) + (-1)(-1) + 3(2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 4 + 1 + 6 = 11 ,</mrow>
</md>
and <m>\|\mathbf n\|^2 = 9</m>, we get
<md>
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \frac{11}{9}\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle .
</md>
Subtracting,
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F_{\parallel} \amp
= \mathbf F - \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle
- \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac49,\ \frac29,\ \frac59\right\rangle
= \frac19\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
As a check, this really is parallel to the plane:
<m>\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= -8 - 2 + 10 = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now let <m>\mathbf d</m> be any allowed unit direction. Because
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> is orthogonal to every
vector lying in the plane,
<md>
W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d
  = \left(\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
    + \mathbf F_{\parallel}\right)\cdot\mathbf d
  = \mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d .
</md>
The normal part of the force is simply wasted: it presses the particle
against the constraint and does no work. Writing
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d
= \|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\|\,\|\mathbf d\|\cos\theta
= \|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\|\cos\theta</m>, the largest value occurs when
<m>\cos\theta = 1</m>, that is when <m>\mathbf d</m> points in the same
direction as <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>. Since
<md>
\left\|\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right\| = \sqrt{16+4+25}
= \sqrt{45} = 3\sqrt5 ,
</md>
the maximizing unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_{\max}
= \frac{\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5} .
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-dp-constrained-work"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-constrained-work">
<caption>
The force <m>\mathbf F</m> splits into a piece
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> along the normal, which
does no work, and a much shorter piece <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
lying in the plane, which is the only part that can do work. The
picture is drawn to scale:
<m>\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F\right\| = \tfrac{11}{3}</m>
while <m>\left\|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right\| = \tfrac{\sqrt5}{3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A tilted plane through the origin with a normal arrow. A force vector
rises steeply from the origin, almost along the normal; it is resolved
into a long component along the normal and a very short component lying
in the plane.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dp-constrained-work">
<diagram dimensions="(600,310)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.7,-1.6,6.0,4.0)">
<polygon at="dpw-plane"
         points="((-0.70,0.30),(5.30,-1.20),(1.90,1.50),(-4.10,3.00))"
         closed="yes"
         stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"
         fill="#0072B2" fill-opacity="0.12"/>
<line at="dpw-dash1" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(1.135,3.034)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dpw-dash2" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(0.166,1.009)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<vector at="dpw-n" v="(0.316,1.261)" tail="(3.3,0.25)"
        stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="dpw-proj" v="(0.535,2.134)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-force" v="(0.101,2.243)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-par" v="(-0.434,0.109)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
<point at="dpw-origin" p="(0.6,0.9)" fill="black"/>
<label at="dpw-originl" anchor="(0.66,0.78)" alignment="se">
<m>O</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-nl" anchor="(3.68,1.56)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf n=\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-forcel" anchor="(0.62,3.24)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-projl" anchor="(1.22,3.06)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-parl" anchor="(0.08,0.97)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-planel" anchor="(5.1,-1.32)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>2x-y+2z=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue parallelogram represents the plane two x minus y plus two z equals zero, drawn through the origin O. A black arrow off to the right shows the normal direction n. From O, a long dark red arrow rises steeply as the projection of F onto n, a slightly tilted orange arrow of almost the same length is the force F itself, and a very short green arrow lying in the plane is the component of F parallel to the plane. Dashed grey segments complete the rectangle of the decomposition.">
  <annotation ref="dpw-plane" text="The constraint plane, two x minus y plus two z equals zero, passing through the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-n" text="A normal vector n to the plane, with components two, negative one, two."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-force" text="The force F, rising steeply and therefore nearly perpendicular to the plane."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-proj" text="The projection of F onto the normal, the long component that does no work."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-par" text="The component of F parallel to the plane, very short compared with the normal component; this is the only part of the force that can do work."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The maximum work per unit displacement is the length of
the in-plane component,
<md>
<mrow>W_{\max} \amp = \left\|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right\|
= \left|\frac19\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right|</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac19\left(3\sqrt5\right) = \frac{\sqrt5}{3}
\approx 0.745 .</mrow>
</md>
We can confirm this directly from the original force:
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_{\max} \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot
  \frac{\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5}
= \frac{-8-2+15}{3\sqrt5}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{5}{3\sqrt5} = \frac{\sqrt5}{3} .</mrow>
</md>
Compare this with <m>\|\mathbf F\| = \sqrt{14} \approx 3.74</m>: the
constraint costs us about <m>80\%</m> of the force.
</p>

<p>
The animation in <xref ref="fig-dp-constrained-work-video"/> runs through
parts (a) and (b) together.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-constrained-work-video">
<caption>
Every unit vector <m>\mathbf d</m> lying in the plane is an allowed
direction, so there are infinitely many of them. The work
<m>W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d
= \left\|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right\|\cos\theta</m>, where
<m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\mathbf d</m> and
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>, is largest when <m>\theta = 0</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-dp-constrained-work"
       source="videos/constrained_work_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/constrained_work_1080p.jpg"
       width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> We want a unit vector
<m>\mathbf d = \left\langle x,y,z\right\rangle</m> satisfying two
conditions at once: it must lie in the plane, and it must do no work.
That is,
<md>
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf n = 0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf F = 0 ,
</md>
or explicitly
<md>
<mrow>2x - y + 2z \amp = 0 ,</mrow>
<mrow>2x - y + 3z \amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives <m>z = 0</m>, and
then <m>2x - y = 0</m>, so <m>y = 2x</m>. Taking <m>x = 1</m> gives the
non-zero vector <m>\left\langle 1,2,0\right\rangle</m>, whose length is
<m>\sqrt{1+4} = \sqrt5</m>. Hence one suitable unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\left\langle 1,\ 2,\ 0\right\rangle .
</md>
Indeed <m>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_0
= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt5}(2 - 2 + 0) = 0</m>, and the opposite direction
<m>-\mathbf d_0</m> works equally well. Geometrically, <m>\mathbf d_0</m>
is the direction in the plane perpendicular to
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>: moving along it, the particle travels
neither with nor against the usable part of the force.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
